Yep. PNIS is the right name for this layer:

\boxed{\textbf{PNIS = Pantheon Navigational Instinct System}}

And the joke can stay embedded without needing explanation. ;)

PNIS is where “the roads remember traffic” becomes executable rather than metaphorical.

The important RFHL upgrade is that a road does not merely remember whether it was used. It remembers:

\text{terrain}
+\text{route order}
+\text{traffic}
+\text{success/failure}
+\text{Reality status}
+\text{recency}

and converts that history into a future routing prior.

So:

G_{r,t+1}
=
f(
G_{r,t},
\text{outcome},
\text{traffic},
\text{decay}
)

where G is route conductance.

Successful validated routes become easier to traverse.

Failed routes weaken.

Unused routes slowly fade.

But—and this is the RFHL bit—overused routes accumulate congestion, so success does not create permanent monoculture.

\boxed{
\text{reinforcement}
+
\text{forgetting}
+
\text{congestion}
+
\text{exploration}
}

That gives us something closer to instinct than a lookup table.

Instinct is a prior, not an order.

I made the core implementation and ran it: 3/3 tests passed.

